WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:09.520
You know guys the term 200 IQ is reserved for a very special type of play

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.840
when it comes to League of Legends.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.280
I'm Colin for theScore esports and this week, we went back to school to give you the

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.000
Top 10 200 IQ Plays in League.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.880
Now for this list guys bear in mind,

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:28.800
we're not talking about mechanical outplays here. Faker-Ryu might have been legendary, but at the end of the day

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.199
came down to raw skill.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.040
For this list we're looking at those more cerebral big brain outplays.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:41.960
So let's get on with it with the Top 10 200 IQ Plays from League of Legends.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.880
Starting off our list at Number 10 is Soaz’s impressive play against SKT.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:55.320
Origen were certainly the underdogs in their 2015 World Championship semifinal match against the Korean

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:59.240
dynasty and needed every edge they could get.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:05.160
Soaz found himself in a tight spot after a gank from Bengi but was able to make a dazzling escape

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.040
thanks to some incredibly quick thinking and perfect timing.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:40.920
(Casting)

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.260
At Number 9 we have Cloud 9’s Impact, who looked to be in a tough spot

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:54.440
after using most of his abilities to avoid a 2v1 gank from I May. With only a third of his health left,

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.840
Impact managed to flash to the safety of his turret.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.120
What happened next was proof of the Korean top laner’s 200 IQ

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.400
as a perfectly placed equalizer allowed him to turn the tables on his opponents.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:20.800
(Casting)

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:27.340
Coming in at Number 8 is Exileh’s timely ultimate during the Summer Split Quarterfinals against Giants.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.040
With a comfortable lead in Game 1, the Unicorns of Love were looking to secure Baron.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.720
But Giants had other plans and attempted to quickly rush the Baron pit.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:44.020
Veritas’ Ashe and Hylisang’s Braum helped keep Giants out of the pit long enough for Move to secure the Baron,

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:50.280
but it was Exileh’s perfectly timed ultimate that allowed the Unicorns to escape with their lives.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:03:17.220
(Casting)

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:23.080
Making its way in at Number 7 is G2’s exceptional teamplay against Fnatic.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:29.840
Faced with a 3v5 situation, it looked like G2 had no way of fending off Fnatic’s 5-man push.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.640
But Perkz managed to turn the tides of the fight

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:38.080
that started with a perfectly placed miasma to punish Jesiz’s forward positioning

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.560
and push caps out of the front line.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:04:03.800
(Casting)

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:07.220
Between Perkz’s instant flash onto mithy’s lantern,

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:11.200
and his last-second heal from twin fangs and dangerous game mastery,

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:17.900
G2 managed to walk away with two kills, only losing Expect at the very end of the fight.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.280
While our Number 6 play may not seem like much from the outset, when dealing with genius

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:26.720
there is always more than meets the eye.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:32.360
Rox had just lost their top laner but noticed an opening after two Afreeca players returned

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:35.880
to the fountain and took the chance to start the Baron.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:41.540
Spirit attempted to get into position to steal the Baron from Rox, but Key had other plans

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:47.199
as he pathed in between Spirit and the Baron, catching the sonic wave and holding onto his

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.080
flash for just the right moment.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:05:09.080
(Casting)

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:13.040
If Key  flashes any sooner, there’s a chance Spirit

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:15.120
doesn’t use his dash.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:21.600
Any later and Spirit would end up in the Baron pit in perfect position to attempt the baron steal.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:26.160
Simply put, this 200 IQ timing secured Rox the Baron.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:31.980
The Number 5 play on our list sees Phoenix 1’s Arrow set up his team

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.800
for a successful teamfight against Team liquid.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:41.000
As Xpecial locks down the enemy Braum, Arrow seizes the opportunity to hit multiple carries,

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:46.560
dodging everything Team Liquid throws at him before cleansing the CC with Mercurial Scimitar

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.760
and flashing back behind his team.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:06:25.920
(Casting)

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:31.240
Because of their positioning and low HP TL couldn’t follow up on the Glacial Fissure

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:34.800
which lead to Phoenix 1 decimating their tanks.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:40.680
Coming in at Number 4 is SKT setting up an impeccable teamfight in Game 3

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:44.560
of the 2017 Mid-Season Invitational finals.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:51.240
Taking on G2, Bang sets up a wall of traps that forced the enemy towards the bottom side of mid lane

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:54.340
while Peanut positioned himself at the top of the lane,

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:57.040
drawing G2’s frontline towards him.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:01.140
With all of their carries on the bottom side of the lane Wolf flashes to the wall,

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:05.920
hits a three man grasping root and follows it up with a stranglethorn.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:28.480
(Casting)

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:30.660
With all of the team fight threats held down,

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:36.760
SKT takeover this fight and ace G2 on their way to defending their MSI title.

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:43.440
Coming in at 3 is a play from the fabled Telecom Wars

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:49.480
that’s unlike any other on our list as it features a number of 200 IQ decisions.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:55.680
Following a picture-perfect teamfight, SKT had to decide where to send their players.

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:59.440
KT’s super minions were beginning to siege their nexus turrets,

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:04.840
the Baron was up, and they had a large wave pushing KT’s mid lane inhibitor turret.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:11.660
The team tasked Wolf and Huni with defending their nexus while the rest of SKT went to push mid.

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:19.880
Huni then teleported to the last surviving minon to help keep SKT’s push alive at the cost of both nexus turrets.

00:08:19.880 --> 00:09:05.600
(Casting)

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:09.200
It proved to be worth it as SKT managed to secure baron,

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:14.400
and win a 5v4 fight under their exposed nexus to secure the win.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:25.000
(Casting)

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:33.280
Our Number 2 play sees SKT against the ropes in Game 2 of their semifinal matchup against The Rox Tigers.

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:36.100
Rox looked to close the game out with PraY and GorillA’s

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:43.360
exceptional pushing ability and managed to take the top inhibitor while Peanut bought them time in the bot lane.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:49.740
Once they had cracked the base, PraY delivered an insane cross map arrow that stopped Duke’s teleport,

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:51.360
and sealed the game.

00:09:51.360 --> 00:10:08.740
(Casting)

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:12.640
With SKT’s return to base staggered and Smeb teleporting in,

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:15.200
the ROX trio had all but secured the win.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:28.160
(Casting)

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:33.580
Coming in at the coveted number one spot is a Fnatic squad

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:39.600
that looked all but finished against SK Gaming at IEM Katowice 2013.

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:46.560
They had super minions knocking on their nexus’ door and had just lost a teamfight in SK’s base

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:52.940
that appeared to be the finishing blow. But xPeke had other plans.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:58.040
After escaping the fight with his life, the legendary mid laner hid in the topside jungle,

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:01.180
waiting for SK to enter his base.

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:07.800
Once they do he teleports back into SK’s base, and the rest quite literally is history.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:30.440
(Casting)

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:34.260
Well guys I don't know about you, but I feel smarter already.

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:38.000
But if any of you braniacs do disagree with our Top 10 list.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:40.600
Well, let me know in the comments, and I'll see you next week.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:48.160
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

